window.DBG_DATA={"env":"$ uname -r\n5.10.104-linuxkit\n$ nproc; python -c 'import os, torch; print(os.cpu_count(), torch.get_num_threads())'\n5\n5 5\n$ cat /sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.max /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.max /sys/fs/cgroup/pids.max\nmax 100000\nmax\nmax\n$ df -h /dev/shm | tail -n 1\nshm              64M     0   64M   0% /dev/shm\n$ ulimit -n\n1048576\n$ grep -E 'MemTotal|SwapTotal' /proc/meminfo\nMemTotal:       10189980 kB\nSwapTotal:       1048572 kB\n$ cat /proc/sys/vm/overcommit_memory\n1\n$ cat /lab/versions.txt /lab/dbg_versions.txt\npython 3.11.2\ntorch 2.14.1+cpu\nnumpy 2.4.6\ntokenizers 0.23.2\npyarrow 25.0.1","envHost":"docker run  kb-os-dbg:1 bash /exp/env.sh","cases":[{"id":"shm","title":"DataLoader workers run out of /dev/shm","sub":["ipc","limits"],"tools":["df","docker"],"ostep":"5 Process API (fork); 13 Address Spaces","cause":"Workers do not send a batch through a pipe byte by byte: they put its tensors in shared memory, files under <code>/dev/shm<\/code> (a tmpfs, a file system kept in RAM), and send only a handle. Two batches in flight per worker (the default <code>prefetch_factor=2<\/code>) of 4096 rows x 256 float32 (4 MiB each) need about 16 MiB; this container's <code>/dev/shm<\/code> is 8 MiB. PyTorch reserves the segment with <code>posix_fallocate<\/code>, so the shortage shows at once as this error (\"No space left on device\", errno 28). Where <code>posix_fallocate<\/code> is not used, the shortage shows later, when the worker writes into the segment and the kernel delivers SIGBUS; PyTorch then reports \"DataLoader worker (pid N) is killed by signal: Bus error. It is possible that dataloader's workers are out of shared memory\" (<a href=\"https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/v2.14.1/aten/src/ATen/MapAllocator.cpp#L290-L312\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">MapAllocator.cpp L290-312<\/a>, <a href=\"https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/v2.14.1/torch/csrc/DataLoader.cpp#L154-L166\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">DataLoader.cpp L154-166<\/a>). The Syscall tracer tab shows the <code>/dev/shm/torch_*<\/code> segments and the POSIX semaphores of the multiprocessing queues living there.","fix":"Give the container a bigger <code>/dev/shm<\/code>: <a href=\"https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/container/run/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">docker run --shm-size<\/a> (the default is 64 MiB, recorded in the environment block above), or in Kubernetes mount an <code>emptyDir<\/code> with <code>medium: Memory<\/code> at <code>/dev/shm<\/code>. With 256 MiB the same job runs. Smaller batches or a lower <code>prefetch_factor<\/code> also reduce the need. The memory used in <code>/dev/shm<\/code> counts against the container's memory limit.","ml":"The classic first failure of a training container: it works on a laptop and dies in Docker or Kubernetes, where /dev/shm is small by default.","symptom":"RuntimeError: unable to allocate shared memory(shm) for file <\/torch_21_3535843693_0>: No space left on device (28)","seen":"","sf":"shm_small","rec":[{"k":"host","f":"shm_small","t":"docker run --cpus 2 --memory 3g --shm-size 8m kb-os-dbg:1 bash /exp/shm.sh 4096"},{"k":"out","f":"shm_small","t":"$ df -h /dev/shm\nFilesystem      Size  Used Avail Use% Mounted on\nshm             8.0M     0  8.0M   0% /dev/shm"},{"k":"out","f":"shm_small","t":"$ python /job/train.py --steps 6 --batch 4096 --workers 2 --start-method fork --no-ckpt > /tmp/log 2>&1; echo exit code $?; grep -E 'time to first|step 5|done at|Error|error' /tmp/log | cut -c1-400 | tail -n 6\nexit code 1\nRuntimeError: Caught RuntimeError in DataLoader worker process 0.\nRuntimeError: unable to allocate shared memory(shm) for file <\/torch_21_3535843693_0>: No space left on device (28)"},{"k":"host","f":"shm_fixed","t":"docker run --cpus 2 --memory 3g --shm-size 256m kb-os-dbg:1 bash /exp/shm.sh 4096"},{"k":"out","f":"shm_fixed","t":"$ python /job/train.py --steps 6 --batch 4096 --workers 2 --start-method fork --no-ckpt > /tmp/log 2>&1; echo exit code $?; grep -E 'time to first|step 5|done at|Error|error' /tmp/log | cut -c1-400 | tail -n 6\nexit code 0\ntime to first batch: 0.075s (start method fork)\nstep 5 loss 2.3046\ndone at step 6 (finished), total 5.553s"}],"first":{"opts":["df -h /dev/shm","nvidia-smi","ulimit -n","free -m"],"a":0,"why":"The message names shared memory and ENOSPC: check the size and use of /dev/shm first."}},{"id":"cow","title":"Worker memory grows: copy-on-write meets reference counts","sub":["memory","process"],"tools":["/proc","ps"],"ostep":"5 Process API; 13 Address Spaces; 21 Swapping: Mechanisms","cause":"After <code>fork<\/code> parent and worker share every page; the kernel copies a page only when one of them writes to it (copy-on-write). Reading a Python object writes to it: CPython increments and decrements the reference count stored in the object's header. A Dataset holding two million Python strings is two million small objects spread over the heap, so a shuffled epoch touches nearly every page and each worker ends up with its own copy: private memory (USS) of worker 0 went from 22 to 197 MiB. The same strings in one numpy buffer or one Arrow buffer are not Python objects, nothing writes to their pages, and USS stays flat (13 MiB for numpy, 11 MiB for Arrow, at the end of the epoch). RSS hides all of this: it stays at 372 MiB per worker, because RSS counts a shared page in full in every process. PSS divides each shared page among its sharers, so the PSS sum is what the job really costs.","fix":"Keep big per-sample metadata in numpy arrays, Arrow tables or pandas columns rather than Python lists or dicts, as the <a href=\"https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/v2.14.1/docs/source/data.md?plain=1#L278-L291\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">PyTorch data docs warn (data.md L278-291)<\/a>. Measure with PSS or USS (<code>/proc/&lt;pid&gt;/smaps_rollup<\/code>, psutil's <code>memory_full_info<\/code>), never by adding RSS.","ml":"Image and caption lists of millions of paths in a Dataset; total memory grows like number of workers x size of those lists until the OOM killer steps in. The OS simulators tab animates copy-on-write page by page and measures the same effect in C.","symptom":"  worker 0        372      256      197","seen":"Memory use climbs during the first epoch and the node or pod runs out, though RSS per worker looks flat. The PSS/USS table shows it (worker 0's row after 500 batches):","sf":"cow_list","rec":[{"k":"host","f":"cow_list","t":"docker run --cpus 2 --memory 3g --shm-size 256m kb-os-dbg:1 bash /exp/cow.sh list"},{"k":"out","f":"cow_list","t":"$ python /exp/cow.py list\ndataset kind: list, 2000000 items, start method fork, 2 workers, batch 1000, shuffled\nafter the first batch\n  process     RSS MiB  PSS MiB  USS MiB\n  main            522      288      167\n  worker 0        371      140       22\n  worker 1        371      139       18\n  sum            1265      567      207\nafter 500 batches\n  process     RSS MiB  PSS MiB  USS MiB\n  main            522      409      352\n  worker 0        372      256      197\n  worker 1        372      256      196\n  sum            1265      920      745\nafter 1000 batches\n  process     RSS MiB  PSS MiB  USS MiB\n  main            522      410      353\n  worker 0        372      256      197\n  worker 1        372      256      197\n  sum            1265      922      748\nafter one epoch (2000 batches, 4.4 s)\n  process     RSS MiB  PSS MiB  USS MiB\n  main            446      334      278\n  worker 0        372      256      197\n  worker 1        372      256      197\n  sum            1189      846      672"},{"k":"out","f":"cow_numpy","t":"$ python /exp/cow.py numpy\ndataset kind: numpy, 2000000 items, start method fork, 2 workers, batch 1000, shuffled\nafter the first batch\n  process     RSS MiB  PSS MiB  USS MiB\n  main            398      243      166\n  worker 0        250       91       11\n  worker 1        250       91       11\n  sum             898      426      187\nafter 500 batches\n  process     RSS MiB  PSS MiB  USS MiB\n  main            398      245      168\n  worker 0        250       93       13\n  worker 1        250       93       13\n  sum             898      432      195\nafter 1000 batches\n  process     RSS MiB  PSS MiB  USS MiB\n  main            398      245      168\n  worker 0        250       93       13\n  worker 1        250       93       13\n  sum             898      432      195\nafter one epoch (2000 batches, 4.6 s)\n  process     RSS MiB  PSS MiB  USS MiB\n  main            323      170       94\n  worker 0        250       93       13\n  worker 1        250       93       13\n  sum             823      357      120"},{"k":"out","f":"cow_arrow","t":"$ python /exp/cow.py arrow\ndataset kind: arrow, 2000000 items, start method fork, 2 workers, batch 1000, shuffled\nafter the first batch\n  process     RSS MiB  PSS MiB  USS MiB\n  main            565      311      184\n  worker 0        398      141       11\n  worker 1        398      141       11\n  sum            1361      593      205\nafter 500 batches\n  process     RSS MiB  PSS MiB  USS MiB\n  main            565      312      185\n  worker 0        398      141       11\n  worker 1        398      141       11\n  sum            1361      594      207\nafter 1000 batches\n  process     RSS MiB  PSS MiB  USS MiB\n  main            565      312      185\n  worker 0        398      141       11\n  worker 1        398      141       11\n  sum            1361      594      207\nafter one epoch (2000 batches, 4.9 s)\n  process     RSS MiB  PSS MiB  USS MiB\n  main            491      238      111\n  worker 0        398      141       11\n  worker 1        398      141       11\n  sum            1286      520      133"}],"first":{"opts":["PSS and USS per worker (smaps_rollup)","RSS per worker in top","nvidia-smi","df -h"],"a":0,"why":"RSS counts shared pages in every process and stays flat here; only USS and PSS show the copies."},"bars":{"title":"Private memory (USS) per worker, MiB: after the first batch, then after one epoch","unit":"MiB","vals":[{"l":"list, start","v":22.0,"g":"22","s":"  worker 0        371      140       22","f":"cow_list"},{"l":"list, end","v":197.0,"g":"197","s":"  worker 0        372      256      197","f":"cow_list"},{"l":"numpy, start","v":11.0,"g":"11","s":"  worker 0        250       91       11","f":"cow_numpy"},{"l":"numpy, end","v":13.0,"g":"13","s":"  worker 0        250       93       13","f":"cow_numpy"},{"l":"arrow, start","v":11.0,"g":"11","s":"  worker 0        398      141       11","f":"cow_arrow"},{"l":"arrow, end","v":11.0,"g":"11","s":"  worker 0        398      141       11","f":"cow_arrow"}]}},{"id":"oom_kill","title":"Killed with exit code 137: the cgroup OOM killer","sub":["memory","limits"],"tools":["cgroup files","dmesg","exit code"],"ostep":"21 Swapping: Mechanisms; 22 Swapping: Policies","cause":"The container's memory cgroup has a hard limit (<code>memory.max<\/code>, 512 MiB here, with no swap). When the processes in it need more and the kernel cannot reclaim enough (page cache, swap), it runs the OOM killer inside that cgroup and sends SIGKILL to one process. SIGKILL cannot be caught, so there is no Python traceback: the shell reports exit status 128 + 9 = 137. If the killed process is the container's main process, the container itself exits with 137. The evidence is in <a href=\"https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/cgroup-v2.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">memory.events<\/a>: <code>oom_kill 1<\/code> (and <code>max<\/code>, the number of times usage hit the limit). The kernel log names the victim, but <code>dmesg<\/code> needs privileges this container does not have (it failed with EPERM); on a node, <code>journalctl -k<\/code> or the node's kernel log has it.","fix":"Find what grows (per-process PSS, see the copy-on-write case; the allocator, see the Reading tab), then lower it or raise the limit. Kubernetes shows the same event as <code>OOMKilled<\/code> in the pod status. On cgroup v2, Kubernetes v1.34 also sets <code>memory.oom.group=1<\/code> on every container (unless the kubelet sets <code>singleProcessOOMKill<\/code>), so one OOM kill takes every process in the container: a DataLoader worker that pushes the container over its limit takes the main process down with it, and the pod reports <code>OOMKilled<\/code> instead of the next case's \"worker exited unexpectedly\" (<a href=\"https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/blob/v1.34.0/pkg/kubelet/kuberuntime/kuberuntime_container_linux.go#L314-L319\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">kuberuntime_container_linux.go L314-319 at v1.34.0<\/a>; <a href=\"https://app.notion.com/p/3f05c17b0d0d8156a841e4bf28f11293\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Containers and isolation<\/a> compares both settings).","ml":"Exit code 137 with no traceback, often only in the worker that died (next case) or in the whole pod. The cgroup counter is the proof; the job's own logs simply stop.","symptom":"exit code 137","seen":"The job's log simply stops; the shell or the orchestrator reports:","sf":"oom_inside","rec":[{"k":"host","f":"oom_inside","t":"docker run --cpus 2 --memory 512m --memory-swap 512m kb-os-dbg:1 bash /exp/oom_inside.sh"},{"k":"out","f":"oom_inside","t":"$ cat /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.max /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.swap.max\n536870912\n0"},{"k":"out","f":"oom_inside","t":"$ python /exp/hog.py 128 2048 | tail -n 3; echo exit code ${PIPESTATUS[0]}\nhog: 128 MiB allocated and touched\nhog: 256 MiB allocated and touched\nhog: 384 MiB allocated and touched\nexit code 137"},{"k":"out","f":"oom_inside","t":"$ cat /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.events\nlow 0\nhigh 0\nmax 38\noom 1\noom_kill 1"},{"k":"out","f":"oom_inside","t":"$ dmesg | tail -n 2\ndmesg: read kernel buffer failed: Operation not permitted"},{"k":"host","f":"oom_pid1","t":"docker run --cpus 2 --memory 512m --memory-swap 512m kb-os-dbg:1 bash /exp/oom_pid1.sh"},{"k":"out","f":"oom_pid1","t":"hog: 128 MiB allocated and touched\nhog: 256 MiB allocated and touched\nhog: 384 MiB allocated and touched"},{"k":"host","f":"oom_pid1","t":"container exit code 137"}],"first":{"opts":["cat memory.events (oom_kill)","py-spy dump","df -h /dev/shm","ulimit -n"],"a":0,"why":"137 = 128 + SIGKILL; memory.events says whether the cgroup OOM killer sent it."}},{"id":"worker_oom","title":"\"DataLoader worker exited unexpectedly\": the OOM killer chose a worker","sub":["memory","process"],"tools":["cgroup files","ps"],"ostep":"5 Process API; 22 Swapping: Policies","cause":"Each worker kept every decoded sample in a dict, so the workers, not the main process, became the largest processes in the 1 GiB cgroup. The OOM killer picks the process with the highest badness score, essentially its resident memory plus swap and page tables (<a href=\"https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v5.10/mm/oom_kill.c#L226-L238\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">oom_kill.c L226-238 at v5.10<\/a>), so it killed a worker. The main process notices that a worker died and raises this error; the message does not say why.","fix":"Do not cache unboundedly in workers (bound the cache, or cache in shared storage). Read <code>memory.events<\/code> to confirm the kill.","ml":"Per-worker caches, decoded-image caches and growing Python lists in <code>__getitem__<\/code> are the usual culprits.","symptom":"RuntimeError: DataLoader worker (pid(s) 14) exited unexpectedly","seen":"","sf":"wkleak","rec":[{"k":"host","f":"wkleak","t":"docker run --cpus 2 --memory 1g --memory-swap 1g --shm-size 256m kb-os-dbg:1 bash /exp/wkleak.sh"},{"k":"out","f":"wkleak","t":"$ python /exp/wkleak.py 2>&1 | tail -n 3\nstep 40\nstep 50\nRuntimeError: DataLoader worker (pid(s) 14) exited unexpectedly\n$ grep oom /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.events\noom 6\noom_kill 1"}],"first":{"opts":["grep oom memory.events","strace -p main","nvidia-smi","py-spy dump"],"a":0,"why":"A worker that dies without its own traceback was killed by a signal; memory.events shows whether it was the OOM killer."}},{"id":"oom_score","title":"Which process the OOM killer picks: oom_score and oom_score_adj","sub":["memory"],"tools":["/proc","cgroup files"],"ostep":"22 Swapping: Policies","cause":"The victim is not necessarily the process that asked for the last page. The badness score is the process's memory in pages plus <code>oom_score_adj<\/code> x (total pages / 1000) (<a href=\"https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v5.10/mm/oom_kill.c#L226-L238\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">oom_kill.c L226-238<\/a>). B held the least memory but had raised its <code>oom_score_adj<\/code> to 500, so B was killed and both A and C, the process whose growth caused the shortage, survived. <code>/proc/&lt;pid&gt;/oom_score<\/code> shows the score (<a href=\"https://docs.kernel.org/filesystems/proc.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">proc docs, oom_score_adj<\/a>). Lowering it needs CAP_SYS_RESOURCE; raising it does not.","fix":"Protect the process that holds the training state (the main process, the one that checkpoints) and let helpers be chosen first: Kubernetes sets <code>oom_score_adj<\/code> from the pod's QoS class.","ml":"In a pod with a sidecar, a data-prep process and the trainer, which one dies first is decided by this score.","symptom":"pid 10 gone","seen":"One process of the job vanished, and it was not the one that was growing:","sf":"oomscore","rec":[{"k":"host","f":"oomscore","t":"docker run --cpus 2 --memory 1g --memory-swap 1g kb-os-dbg:1 bash /exp/oomscore.sh"},{"k":"out","f":"oomscore","t":"$ cat /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.max /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.swap.max\n1073741824\n0"},{"k":"out","f":"oomscore","t":"$ for p in 9 10; do echo pid $p: $(tr '\\0' ' ' < /proc/$p/cmdline | cut -c1-40) oom_score_adj=$(cat /proc/$p/oom_score_adj) oom_score=$(cat /proc/$p/oom_score) rss_kib=$(grep VmRSS /proc/$p/status | tr -s ' ' | cut -d' ' -f2); done\npid 9: python /exp/hog.py 150 450 600 A oom_score_adj=0 oom_score=695 rss_kib=484820\npid 10: python /exp/hog.py 125 250 600 B oom_score_adj=500 oom_score=1016 rss_kib=280084"},{"k":"out","f":"oomscore","t":"$ python /exp/hog.py 50 400 2 C | tail -n 2\nC: 400 MiB allocated and touched\nC: done\n/exp/lib.sh: line 5:    10 Killed                  sh -c 'echo 500 > /proc/self/oom_score_adj; exec python /exp/hog.py 125 250 600 B' > /tmp/B.log 2>&1"},{"k":"out","f":"oomscore","t":"$ for p in 9 10; do if kill -0 $p 2>/dev/null; then echo pid $p alive; else echo pid $p gone; fi; done; tail -n 1 /tmp/A.log /tmp/B.log\npid 9 alive\npid 10 gone\n==> /tmp/A.log <==\nA: 450 MiB allocated and touched\n\n==> /tmp/B.log <==\nB: 250 MiB allocated and touched\n$ grep oom /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.events\noom 1\noom_kill 1"}],"first":{"opts":["cat /proc/<pid>/oom_score for each process","top sorted by CPU","ulimit -a","df -h"],"a":0,"why":"The score, not the latest allocation, decides the victim."}},{"id":"memerr","title":"MemoryError and \"can't allocate\": a refusal, not a kill","sub":["memory","limits"],"tools":["/proc","ulimit"],"ostep":"14 Memory API; 13 Address Spaces","cause":"A Python <code>MemoryError<\/code> (and numpy's and PyTorch's equivalents) means an allocation call returned failure, so the program can catch it and print a traceback. That happens when the kernel refuses the request up front. How readily it refuses is the overcommit policy (<a href=\"https://docs.kernel.org/mm/overcommit-accounting.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">overcommit accounting<\/a>): this VM runs mode 1 (always grant), so even a 1 TiB <code>np.zeros<\/code> succeeds, with 1 TiB of virtual size and 23380 kB resident, because no page has been touched. Under an address-space limit (<code>ulimit -v<\/code>, RLIMIT_AS, <a href=\"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/getrlimit.2.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">getrlimit(2)<\/a>) the same requests fail immediately with the three messages shown. Compare the OOM-kill case: there the allocation succeeded and the process died later, on touching the memory, with no traceback.","fix":"Read the size in the message: a nonsense size (terabytes) is a shape bug; a plausible one means a real limit (RLIMIT_AS, mode 2 overcommit, or a 32-bit build).","ml":"A traceback means the allocator said no; exit code 137 without a traceback means the kernel said yes and then killed the process.","symptom":"numpy._core._exceptions._ArrayMemoryError: Unable to allocate 12.0 GiB for an array with shape (12884901888,) and data type uint8","seen":"","sf":"memerr","rec":[{"k":"host","f":"memerr","t":"docker run --cpus 2 --memory 3g kb-os-dbg:1 bash /exp/memerr.sh"},{"k":"out","f":"memerr","t":"$ cat /proc/sys/vm/overcommit_memory\n1"},{"k":"out","f":"memerr","t":"$ python -c \"import numpy as np; a = np.zeros(1 << 40, dtype=np.uint8); print('allocated', a.nbytes >> 30, 'GiB'); print(''.join(l for l in open('/proc/self/status') if l.startswith(('VmSize', 'VmRSS'))), end='')\"\nallocated 1024 GiB\nVmSize:\t1073997084 kB\nVmRSS:\t   23380 kB"},{"k":"out","f":"memerr","t":"$ ulimit -v 8388608; python -c 'x = bytearray(12 << 30)' 2>&1 | tail -n 1\nMemoryError\n$ ulimit -v 8388608; python -c 'import numpy as np; x = np.ones(12 << 30, dtype=np.uint8)' 2>&1 | tail -n 1\nnumpy._core._exceptions._ArrayMemoryError: Unable to allocate 12.0 GiB for an array with shape (12884901888,) and data type uint8\n$ ulimit -v 8388608; python -c 'import torch; x = torch.empty(12 << 30, dtype=torch.uint8)' 2>&1 | tail -n 1 | cut -c1-300\nRuntimeError: [enforce fail at alloc_cpu.cpp:113] data. DefaultCPUAllocator: not enough memory: you tried to allocate 12884901888 bytes."}],"first":{"opts":["ulimit -v and overcommit_memory","memory.events","nvidia-smi","py-spy dump"],"a":0,"why":"A traceback with MemoryError is a refused request: check what limits requests."}},{"id":"cuda_oom","title":"CUDA out of memory (from source; not run here: no GPU)","sub":["memory","gpu"],"tools":["nvidia-smi","torch.cuda.memory_summary()"],"ostep":"17 Free Space Management","cause":"GPU memory is not managed by the Linux kernel's OOM killer: PyTorch's caching allocator asks the CUDA driver for memory, keeps freed blocks for reuse, and raises a catchable <code>torch.OutOfMemoryError<\/code> when even after returning its cached blocks the request cannot be met. The message, built in the source above (<a href=\"https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/v2.14.1/c10/cuda/CUDACachingAllocator.cpp#L1927-L1952\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CUDACachingAllocator.cpp L1927-1952 at v2.14.1<\/a>), splits the memory into allocated by PyTorch, reserved by PyTorch but unallocated (cached), and free on the device. A large reserved-but-unallocated figure means fragmentation: free memory exists but not as one block big enough, the \"out of memory but memory is free\" story of the Reading tab.","fix":"As the message itself says: try <code>PYTORCH_CUDA_ALLOC_CONF=expandable_segments:True<\/code> against fragmentation (<a href=\"https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/notes/cuda.html#optimizing-memory-usage-with-pytorch-cuda-alloc-conf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">PyTorch CUDA memory notes<\/a>); otherwise reduce batch size or activation memory (checkpointing, mixed precision). <code>torch.cuda.memory_summary()<\/code> prints the breakdown.","ml":"Not reproducible in this VM, which has no GPU; the source and the docs are the evidence. It differs from both CPU cases above: a Python exception, not a kill, and not a kernel refusal either.","symptom":"\"CUDA out of memory. Tried to allocate \",","seen":"On a GPU, from the source that builds the message (not run here):","sf":"@excerpts","rec":[{"k":"src","f":"@excerpts","t":"      TORCH_CHECK_WITH(\n          OutOfMemoryError,\n          false,\n          \"CUDA out of memory. Tried to allocate \",\n          format_size(alloc_size),\n          \". GPU \",\n          static_cast<int>(device_id),\n          \" has a total capacity of \",\n          format_size(device_total),\n          \" of which \",\n          format_size(device_free),\n          \" is free. \",\n          proc_info,\n          allowed_info,\n          \"Of the allocated memory \",\n          format_size(allocated_bytes + allocated_in_private_pools),\n          \" is allocated by PyTorch, \",\n          private_pool_msg,\n          \"and \",\n          format_size(\n              reserved_bytes - allocated_bytes - allocated_in_private_pools),\n          \" is reserved by PyTorch but unallocated.\",\n          CUDAAllocatorConfig::expandable_segments()\n              ? \"\"\n              : \" If reserved but unallocated memory is large try setting\"\n                \" PYTORCH_CUDA_ALLOC_CONF=expandable_segments:True to avoid\""}],"first":{"opts":["torch.cuda.memory_summary() (reserved vs allocated)","free -m","memory.events","ulimit -v"],"a":0,"why":"The GPU allocator is PyTorch's, not the kernel's: its own summary separates fragmentation from real exhaustion."}},{"id":"swap","title":"Not dead, just slow: the working set spills into swap","sub":["memory","limits"],"tools":["cgroup files","getrusage"],"ostep":"21 Swapping: Mechanisms; 22 Swapping: Policies","cause":"With swap allowed (<code>memory.swap.max<\/code> 512 MiB here), a working set of 700 MiB in a 512 MiB limit is not killed: the kernel writes the least recently used pages to swap and reads them back on access. Each read back is a major page fault, a fault the kernel can only satisfy by doing I/O. A pass over the 700 MiB array cost 23674 major faults and 11.66 s; the same pass over 300 MiB, which fits, printed 0.00 s (under 10 ms). <code>memory.events<\/code> counts how often usage hit <code>max<\/code>, but no <code>oom_kill<\/code>.","fix":"Size the job to fit (or raise the limit); for training, prefer failing fast: Kubernetes nodes usually run with swap off, and <code>--memory-swap<\/code> equal to <code>--memory<\/code> disables it for a container.","ml":"A step time that suddenly grows tenfold with the CPU mostly idle and major faults climbing.","symptom":"700 MiB working set, pass 1:  11.66 s, major faults 23674, swap in use 255 MiB","seen":"Steps become many times slower, the CPU is mostly idle, nothing crashes:","sf":"swap","rec":[{"k":"host","f":"swap","t":"docker run --cpus 2 --memory 512m --memory-swap 1g kb-os-dbg:1 bash /exp/swap.sh"},{"k":"out","f":"swap","t":"$ cat /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.max /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.swap.max\n536870912\n536870912"},{"k":"out","f":"swap","t":"$ python /exp/swap.py 300\n300 MiB working set, pass 0:   0.06 s, major faults 0, swap in use 0 MiB\n300 MiB working set, pass 1:   0.00 s, major faults 0, swap in use 0 MiB\n300 MiB working set, pass 2:   0.00 s, major faults 0, swap in use 0 MiB\n$ python /exp/swap.py 700\n700 MiB working set, pass 0:   9.63 s, major faults 19707, swap in use 255 MiB\n700 MiB working set, pass 1:  11.66 s, major faults 23674, swap in use 255 MiB\n700 MiB working set, pass 2:  10.38 s, major faults 23837, swap in use 255 MiB\n$ grep -E '^(max|oom_kill)' /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.events\nmax 14242\noom_kill 0"}],"first":{"opts":["major faults (ps -o maj_flt, getrusage) and memory.swap.current","nvidia-smi","ulimit -n","df -h /dev/shm"],"a":0,"why":"Slow with an idle CPU and rising major faults points to paging."},"bars":{"title":"Seconds per pass over the array (touch every page once)","unit":"s","vals":[{"l":"300 MiB fits","v":0.0,"g":"0.00","s":"300 MiB working set, pass 1:   0.00 s","f":"swap"},{"l":"700 MiB in 512 MiB","v":11.66,"g":"11.66","s":"700 MiB working set, pass 1:  11.66 s","f":"swap"}]}},{"id":"throttle","title":"Step time quadruples under a CPU limit: cgroup throttling","sub":["cpu","limits"],"tools":["cgroup files","getrusage"],"ostep":"7 CPU Scheduling; 9 Lottery Scheduling","cause":"A CPU limit is a quota, not fewer CPUs: <code>cpu.max<\/code> = 50000 100000 lets the cgroup run 50 ms of CPU time in every 100 ms period, on any CPUs (<a href=\"https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/cgroup-v2.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">cgroup v2 cpu.max<\/a>). Two threads burn the 50 ms in about 25 ms of wall time and then the whole cgroup is stopped until the next period: <code>cpu.stat<\/code> shows it throttled in 140 of 140 periods, 20621276 microseconds throttled in a 14.00 s run (the throttled time adds up over threads). The same work took 138 ms per step with 2 CPUs and 701 ms with half a CPU.","fix":"Give the job the CPUs it uses, or use fewer threads than the quota allows (next case). In Kubernetes a CPU limit becomes exactly this quota (<a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/manage-resources-containers/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">resource limits<\/a>); some clusters set requests without limits for training pods for this reason.","ml":"DataLoader workers, the main loop and the framework's thread pool all share the pod's quota; a GPU waits while its feeding CPU is throttled. <code>nr_throttled<\/code> rising is the signature.","symptom":"step time: median 701 ms","seen":"Steps are several times slower than on a laptop with the same number of CPUs:","sf":"cpu_half","rec":[{"k":"host","f":"cpu_2","t":"docker run --cpus 2 --memory 3g kb-os-dbg:1 bash /exp/cpu.sh 2"},{"k":"out","f":"cpu_2","t":"$ python /exp/cpu.py 2 20\ncpu.max: 200000 100000 (2 CPUs); os.cpu_count() = 5; torch threads = 2\nstep time: median 138 ms, slowest 189 ms, total 2.80 s for 20 steps\ncpu.stat during the run: nr_periods 28, nr_throttled 2, throttled_usec 8032, usage_usec 5538383\ncontext switches: voluntary 126, involuntary 193"},{"k":"host","f":"cpu_half","t":"docker run --cpus 0.5 --memory 3g kb-os-dbg:1 bash /exp/cpu.sh 2"},{"k":"out","f":"cpu_half","t":"$ python /exp/cpu.py 2 20\ncpu.max: 50000 100000 (0.5 CPUs); os.cpu_count() = 5; torch threads = 2\nstep time: median 701 ms, slowest 1106 ms, total 14.00 s for 20 steps\ncpu.stat during the run: nr_periods 140, nr_throttled 140, throttled_usec 20621276, usage_usec 7002875\ncontext switches: voluntary 133, involuntary 926"}],"first":{"opts":["cat cpu.stat (nr_throttled, throttled_usec)","free -m","df -h","ulimit -n"],"a":0,"why":"Slow steps with a CPU limit: the cgroup's own counters say whether it is being throttled."},"bars":{"title":"Median step time, ms (same work)","unit":"ms","vals":[{"l":"2 CPUs, 2 threads","v":138.0,"g":"138","s":"step time: median 138 ms","f":"cpu_2"},{"l":"0.5 CPU, 2 threads","v":701.0,"g":"701","s":"step time: median 701 ms","f":"cpu_half"}]}},{"id":"threads","title":"Too many threads for the CPUs you have: os.cpu_count() sees the whole machine","sub":["cpu","limits"],"tools":["cgroup files","getrusage"],"ostep":"10 Multi-CPU Scheduling; 26 Concurrency and Threads","cause":"A CPU quota does not change what the process sees: <code>os.cpu_count()<\/code> and <code>nproc<\/code> report the machine's 5 CPUs, and PyTorch sizes its intra-op thread pool from them (<a href=\"https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.set_num_threads.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">torch.set_num_threads<\/a>). Five threads on a one-CPU quota take turns, wait for each other at every parallel region, and are throttled in every period: 496 ms per step, against 210 ms with one thread doing the same work. 3571 involuntary context switches (the kernel taking the CPU away, against 16 with one thread) and 11334075 microseconds of CPU charged (against 4316862) for the same arithmetic are the cost of the contention.","fix":"Set the thread count from the quota, not from the machine: <code>torch.set_num_threads(n)<\/code> or <code>OMP_NUM_THREADS<\/code>, with n = <code>cpu.max<\/code> quota / period (or the Kubernetes CPU limit); count DataLoader workers in the same budget. Python 3.13 added <code>os.process_cpu_count()<\/code>, which honours CPU affinity but not a quota.","ml":"The common Kubernetes case: a pod limited to 4 CPUs on a 128-core node starts 128 threads per process.","symptom":"os.cpu_count() = 5; torch threads = 5","seen":"Steps are slow on a small CPU limit, and the job reports more threads than it has CPUs:","sf":"cpu_one","rec":[{"k":"host","f":"cpu_one","t":"docker run --cpus 1 --memory 3g kb-os-dbg:1 bash /exp/cpu.sh 0 1"},{"k":"out","f":"cpu_one","t":"$ python /exp/cpu.py 0 20\ncpu.max: 100000 100000 (1 CPUs); os.cpu_count() = 5; torch threads = 5\nstep time: median 496 ms, slowest 997 ms, total 11.31 s for 20 steps\ncpu.stat during the run: nr_periods 113, nr_throttled 113, throttled_usec 40357317, usage_usec 11334075\ncontext switches: voluntary 948, involuntary 3571"},{"k":"out","f":"cpu_one","t":"$ python /exp/cpu.py 1 20\ncpu.max: 100000 100000 (1 CPUs); os.cpu_count() = 5; torch threads = 1\nstep time: median 210 ms, slowest 267 ms, total 4.33 s for 20 steps\ncpu.stat during the run: nr_periods 43, nr_throttled 12, throttled_usec 14277, usage_usec 4316862\ncontext switches: voluntary 0, involuntary 16"}],"first":{"opts":["compare torch.get_num_threads() with cpu.max","nvidia-smi","df -h","cat memory.events"],"a":0,"why":"The thread count comes from the machine, the CPU time from the quota: compare the two."},"bars":{"title":"Median step time on 1 CPU, ms (same work)","unit":"ms","vals":[{"l":"5 threads (default)","v":496.0,"g":"496","s":"step time: median 496 ms","f":"cpu_one"},{"l":"1 thread","v":210.0,"g":"210","s":"step time: median 210 ms","f":"cpu_one"}]}},{"id":"starve","title":"The training loop waits for data: DataLoader-bound","sub":["cpu","process"],"tools":["getrusage","py-spy"],"ostep":"26 Concurrency and Threads","cause":"With <code>num_workers=0<\/code> the main process decodes every sample itself, so the model step (here a fixed matmul standing in for the GPU's work, since this VM has none) waits 90% of the time. <a href=\"https://github.com/benfred/py-spy\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">py-spy<\/a> reads the interpreter's stack from outside without stopping it (it needs the ptrace permission, added to this container with CAP_SYS_PTRACE) and shows the main thread inside <code>__getitem__<\/code> and the FFT, not in the model. Workers move that work to other processes and CPUs; on this 2-CPU container the step time falls from 40 ms to 23 ms with 2 workers and 27 ms with 4: beyond the CPUs the container has, more workers only compete for them.","fix":"More workers up to the CPUs the job really has (the quota, see the previous case); cheaper decoding; preprocessing offline. Measure the time spent in <code>next(loader)<\/code> before buying a bigger GPU.","ml":"Low GPU utilisation with a busy CPU is this case until proven otherwise.","symptom":"num_workers=0: 40 ms per step; waiting for data 90%, computing 10%","seen":"Low GPU utilisation; the loop's own timing says:","sf":"starve","rec":[{"k":"host","f":"starve","t":"docker run --cpus 2 --memory 3g --cap-add SYS_PTRACE kb-os-dbg:1 bash /exp/starve.sh"},{"k":"out","f":"starve","t":"$ python /exp/starve.py 0\nnum_workers=0: 40 ms per step; waiting for data 90%, computing 10%\n$ python /exp/starve.py 1\nnum_workers=1: 40 ms per step; waiting for data 88%, computing 12%\n$ python /exp/starve.py 2\nnum_workers=2: 23 ms per step; waiting for data 79%, computing 21%\n$ python /exp/starve.py 4\nnum_workers=4: 27 ms per step; waiting for data 65%, computing 35%"},{"k":"out","f":"starve","t":"$ py-spy dump --pid 54 2>&1 | head -n 12\nProcess 54: python /exp/starve.py 0 400\nPython v3.11.2 (/usr/bin/python3.11)\n\nThread 54 (active): \"MainThread\"\n    _raw_fft (numpy/fft/_pocketfft.py:101)\n    rfft (numpy/fft/_pocketfft.py:417)\n    __getitem__ (starve.py:21)\n    <listcomp> (torch/utils/data/_utils/fetch.py:54)\n    fetch (torch/utils/data/_utils/fetch.py:54)\n    _next_data (torch/utils/data/dataloader.py:785)\n    __next__ (torch/utils/data/dataloader.py:725)\n    <module> (starve.py:32)"}],"first":{"opts":["py-spy dump of the main process","df -h /dev/shm","cat memory.events","ulimit -n"],"a":0,"why":"Where the main thread is waiting tells you whether the input pipeline is the bottleneck."},"bars":{"title":"Milliseconds per step by number of DataLoader workers (2 CPUs)","unit":"ms","vals":[{"l":"0 workers","v":40.0,"g":"40","s":"num_workers=0: 40 ms","f":"starve"},{"l":"1 worker","v":40.0,"g":"40","s":"num_workers=1: 40 ms","f":"starve"},{"l":"2 workers","v":23.0,"g":"23","s":"num_workers=2: 23 ms","f":"starve"},{"l":"4 workers","v":27.0,"g":"27","s":"num_workers=4: 27 ms","f":"starve"}]}},{"id":"pids","title":"BlockingIOError on fork: the pids limit counts threads too","sub":["process","limits"],"tools":["/proc","cgroup files"],"ostep":"5 Process API; 27 Thread API","cause":"<code>pids.max<\/code> limits tasks, and on Linux every thread is a task. The running job with 2 workers already uses 16 (the main process alone has 5 threads). With 16 workers and 4 threads each the cgroup reaches its limit of 48, and <code>fork<\/code> (<code>clone<\/code>) fails with EAGAIN, which Python raises as <code>BlockingIOError<\/code>; <code>pids.events<\/code> counts the refusals. A thread that cannot be created fails the same way (\"can't start new thread\").","fix":"Raise the limit (<a href=\"https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/container/run/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">docker --pids-limit<\/a>, Kubernetes <code>podPidsLimit<\/code>) or start fewer workers and threads.","ml":"Large worker counts times the thread pools of numpy, OpenMP and tokenizers in every worker.","symptom":"BlockingIOError: [Errno 11] Resource temporarily unavailable","seen":"","sf":"pids","rec":[{"k":"host","f":"pids","t":"docker run --cpus 2 --memory 3g --shm-size 256m --pids-limit 48 kb-os-dbg:1 bash /exp/pids.sh"},{"k":"out","f":"pids","t":"$ cat /sys/fs/cgroup/pids.max\n48"},{"k":"out","f":"pids","t":"$ cat /sys/fs/cgroup/pids.current; for p in $(pgrep -f train.py); do echo pid $p threads $(ls /proc/$p/task | wc -l); done\n16\npid 14 threads 5\npid 2356 threads 4\npid 2358 threads 4\npid 2600 threads 1"},{"k":"out","f":"pids","t":"$ python /job/train.py --steps 5 --workers 16 --threads 4 --start-method fork --no-ckpt > /tmp/log 2>&1; echo exit code $?; grep -E 'Error|error|done at|os.fork' /tmp/log | cut -c1-200 | tail -n 4\nexit code 1\n    self.pid = os.fork()\nBlockingIOError: [Errno 11] Resource temporarily unavailable\n$ cat /sys/fs/cgroup/pids.events\nmax 3"}],"first":{"opts":["cat pids.max pids.current pids.events","free -m","df -h","nvidia-smi"],"a":0,"why":"EAGAIN from fork in a container is usually the pids controller."}},{"id":"zombie","title":"Zombies pile up when the training script is PID 1","sub":["process","signal"],"tools":["ps"],"ostep":"5 Process API","cause":"A process that exits stays in the process table as a zombie (state Z, \"defunct\") until its parent collects its exit status with <code>wait<\/code>. If its parent died first, it is handed to PID 1 of its PID namespace, whose job is to reap such orphans. In a container started with the training script as its command, PID 1 is the training script, which never calls <code>wait<\/code> for children it did not start: the orphaned <code>sleep<\/code> stays a zombie. Zombies use no memory but each holds a PID, which counts against <code>pids.max<\/code>.","fix":"Run a real init as PID 1: <a href=\"https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/container/run/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">docker run --init<\/a> (a small init, <a href=\"https://github.com/krallin/tini\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">tini<\/a>) reaps orphans, as the second run shows (PID 1 is <code>docker-init<\/code> and no zombie remains). In Kubernetes, use an init in the image or share the pod's process namespace.","ml":"Launch scripts that background helpers (log shippers, monitors) inside a training container.","symptom":"    8     1 Z    sleep","seen":"ps in the container lists processes in state Z that never go away:","sf":"zombie","rec":[{"k":"host","f":"zombie","t":"docker run --cpus 1 --memory 1g kb-os-dbg:1 bash /exp/zombie.sh"},{"k":"out","f":"zombie","t":"I am pid 1\nhelper started pid 8\n  PID  PPID STAT COMMAND\n    1     0 Ss   python\n    8     1 Z    sleep\n    9     1 R    ps"},{"k":"host","f":"zombie_init","t":"docker run --cpus 1 --memory 1g --init kb-os-dbg:1 bash /exp/zombie.sh"},{"k":"out","f":"zombie_init","t":"I am pid 7\nhelper started pid 9\n  PID  PPID STAT COMMAND\n    1     0 Ss   docker-init\n    7     1 S    python\n   10     7 R    ps"}],"first":{"opts":["ps -eo pid,ppid,stat,comm (look for Z and PPID 1)","free -m","cat cpu.stat","df -h"],"a":0,"why":"State Z with parent 1 means nobody is reaping."}},{"id":"orphan","title":"The main process is SIGKILLed: what happens to its workers","sub":["process","signal"],"tools":["ps"],"ostep":"5 Process API","cause":"SIGKILL gives the main process no chance to stop its workers. They become orphans, re-parented to PID 1 (here the container's shell), and keep running. DataLoader workers check every <a href=\"https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/v2.14.1/torch/utils/data/_utils/__init__.py#L19\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">MP_STATUS_CHECK_INTERVAL = 5.0 s<\/a> whether their parent is still alive and exit when it is not, so they were gone within seven seconds. Child processes that have no such watchdog (your own multiprocessing pools, subprocesses) keep running and keep their memory and GPU.","fix":"Prefer SIGTERM with a handler (the SIGTERM cases); make your own children check their parent or put them in the same process group; on Linux <code>prctl(PR_SET_PDEATHSIG)<\/code> asks the kernel to signal a child when its parent dies.","ml":"After an OOM kill of the main process, leftover workers can hold memory and, on GPU nodes, device memory, making the restart fail.","symptom":" 3133     1 Sl   python /job/train.py --steps 100000 --workers 2 --start-method ","seen":"After the main process was killed, ps still lists its workers, now with parent PID 1:","sf":"orphan","rec":[{"k":"host","f":"orphan","t":"docker run --cpus 2 --memory 3g --shm-size 256m kb-os-dbg:1 bash /exp/orphan.sh"},{"k":"out","f":"orphan","t":"$ ps -o pid,ppid,stat,etime,cmd --ppid 1 --ppid 13 | cut -c1-80 | grep -v ' ps '\n  PID  PPID STAT     ELAPSED CMD\n   13     1 Rl         00:08 python /job/train.py --steps 100000 --workers 2 --s\n 3133    13 Sl         00:00 python /job/train.py --steps 100000 --workers 2 --s\n 3135    13 Sl         00:00 python /job/train.py --steps 100000 --workers 2 --s"},{"k":"out","f":"orphan","t":"$ ps -eo pid,ppid,stat,cmd | grep -E 'train.py|PID' | grep -v grep | cut -c1-80\n/exp/orphan.sh: line 9:    13 Killed                  python /job/train.py --steps 100000 --workers 2 --start-method fork --no-ckpt > /tmp/log 2>&1\n  PID  PPID STAT CMD\n 3133     1 Sl   python /job/train.py --steps 100000 --workers 2 --start-method \n 3135     1 Sl   python /job/train.py --steps 100000 --workers 2 --start-method "},{"k":"out","f":"orphan","t":"$ ps -eo pid,ppid,stat,cmd | grep -E 'train.py|PID' | grep -v grep | cut -c1-80\n  PID  PPID STAT CMD"}],"first":{"opts":["ps -eo pid,ppid,stat,cmd (PPID 1 orphans)","df -h","ulimit -n","cat cpu.stat"],"a":0,"why":"Orphans show as PPID 1 with the job's command line."}},{"id":"sig_pid1","title":"docker stop takes 10 s and ends in 137: SIGTERM ignored by PID 1","sub":["signal","process"],"tools":["docker","exit code"],"ostep":"5 Process API","cause":"<code>docker stop<\/code> sends SIGTERM, waits (10 s by default) and then sends SIGKILL (<a href=\"https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/container/stop/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">docker stop<\/a>); Kubernetes does the same with a 30 s grace period (<a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/pod-lifecycle/#pod-termination\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">pod termination<\/a>). A process with no SIGTERM handler normally dies at once (default action: terminate). But this script is PID 1 of the container's PID namespace, and the kernel delivers to PID 1 only the signals it has installed a handler for (<a href=\"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/pid_namespaces.7.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">pid_namespaces(7)<\/a>, <a href=\"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/kill.2.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">kill(2) NOTES<\/a>). So SIGTERM did nothing, the job kept training for 10 s, and SIGKILL (which PID 1 cannot block from an ancestor namespace) ended it: exit 137, everything since the last periodic checkpoint lost.","fix":"Install a SIGTERM handler that checkpoints and exits (the handler case below), or run under an init (<code>--init<\/code>) that forwards signals.","ml":"Preemptible and spot instances, Slurm and Kubernetes all announce the end with SIGTERM; a job that ignores it loses the grace period it was given to save its state.","symptom":"took 10.5 s; container exit code 137","seen":"Stopping the job always takes the full grace period, and it exits with 137:","sf":"sig_noh","rec":[{"k":"host","f":"sig_noh","t":"docker run --cpus 2 --memory 3g kb-os-dbg:1 bash /exp/sig.sh noh"},{"k":"out","f":"sig_noh","t":"pid 1 training\nstep 500: checkpoint saved\nstep 1000: checkpoint saved\nstep 1500: checkpoint saved\nstep 2000: checkpoint saved"},{"k":"host","f":"sig_noh","t":"docker stop (SIGTERM, then SIGKILL after 10 s) took 10.5 s; container exit code 137"}],"first":{"opts":["does PID 1 have a SIGTERM handler? (grep SigCgt /proc/1/status)","free -m","df -h","cat cpu.stat"],"a":0,"why":"A 10 s stop is the grace period expiring: the signal was ignored or not forwarded."},"bars":{"title":"Seconds from docker stop to exit, and the exit code","unit":"s","vals":[{"l":"no handler, PID 1","v":10.5,"g":"10.5","s":"took 10.5 s","f":"sig_noh"},{"l":"no handler, --init","v":0.5,"g":"0.5","s":"took 0.5 s","f":"sig_noh_init"},{"l":"handler, PID 1","v":1.0,"g":"1.0","s":"took 1.0 s","f":"sig_train"},{"l":"handler, shell is PID 1","v":10.7,"g":"10.7","s":"took 10.7 s","f":"sig_wrapper"}]}},{"id":"sig_default","title":"Stopped at once with 143 and no checkpoint: SIGTERM's default action","sub":["signal"],"tools":["docker","exit code"],"ostep":"5 Process API","cause":"Under <code>--init<\/code> the script is not PID 1, so SIGTERM gets its default action: the process is terminated immediately, exit status 128 + 15 = 143. Fast, but the job saved nothing after its last periodic checkpoint.","fix":"A handler that sets a flag, lets the current step finish, checkpoints and exits 0.","ml":"143 in a pod's status means it was asked to stop and did not handle it.","symptom":"container exit code 143","seen":"Stopped instantly, but the last checkpoint is old:","sf":"sig_noh_init","rec":[{"k":"host","f":"sig_noh_init","t":"docker run --cpus 2 --memory 3g --init kb-os-dbg:1 bash /exp/sig.sh noh"},{"k":"out","f":"sig_noh_init","t":"pid 7 training\nstep 500: checkpoint saved"},{"k":"host","f":"sig_noh_init","t":"docker stop (SIGTERM, then SIGKILL after 10 s) took 0.5 s; container exit code 143"}],"first":{"opts":["is there a SIGTERM handler? (signal.getsignal)","memory.events","df -h","ulimit -n"],"a":0,"why":"143 = 128 + SIGTERM: the default action ran."}},{"id":"sig_handler","title":"The fix: a SIGTERM handler that checkpoints (the running job)","sub":["signal","storage"],"tools":["docker","logs"],"ostep":"5 Process API; 42 FSCK and Journaling","cause":"The running job's handler only sets a flag (a Python signal handler runs in the main thread between bytecodes, so it should do as little as possible); the loop sees the flag after the current step, writes the checkpoint with the write-then-rename pattern and exits 0, 1.0 s after <code>docker stop<\/code>. The handler works as PID 1 because a handler is installed. This run was untraced. The Syscall tracer tab found that under strace the signal can be delivered to another thread first, so Python's handler runs one step later; untraced runs stopped on time.","fix":"This is the pattern: flag in the handler, checkpoint in the loop, exit well inside the grace period.","ml":"Graceful preemption. On multi-GPU jobs every rank must agree to stop at the same step before saving.","symptom":"got signal 15; will checkpoint and exit","seen":"The good outcome, in the job's log:","sf":"sig_train","rec":[{"k":"host","f":"sig_train","t":"docker run --cpus 2 --memory 3g --shm-size 256m kb-os-dbg:1 bash /exp/sig.sh train"},{"k":"out","f":"sig_train","t":"step 850 loss 0.1217\nstep 855 loss 0.1747\nstep 860 loss 0.1769\n[   9.313s] got signal 15; will checkpoint and exit\n[   9.314s] phase ckpt_begin\n[   9.327s] phase ckpt_end\n[   9.344s] phase exit\ndone at step 862 (SIGTERM), total 9.344s"},{"k":"host","f":"sig_train","t":"docker stop (SIGTERM, then SIGKILL after 10 s) took 1.0 s; container exit code 0"}],"first":{"opts":["the job's log around the signal","nvidia-smi","ulimit -n","df -h"],"a":0,"why":"Confirm the handler ran and the checkpoint finished before the grace period."}},{"id":"sig_wrapper","title":"A handler that never runs: the launcher shell does not forward SIGTERM","sub":["signal","process"],"tools":["docker","ps"],"ostep":"5 Process API","cause":"Here the container's command is a shell script that runs <code>python train.py<\/code> as a child (piping its output). The shell is PID 1, receives SIGTERM, has no handler, so the kernel drops it; the shell does not pass it on, and Python, whose handler would have saved a checkpoint, never hears of it. After 10 s SIGKILL ends everything: exit 137, no checkpoint, although the job has a perfect handler.","fix":"<code>exec python train.py<\/code> as the last line of the script (the shell is replaced, Python becomes PID 1), or <code>--init<\/code>/tini, which forwards signals to its child, or a shell <code>trap<\/code> that forwards the signal.","ml":"Entry-point scripts that set up the environment and then call Python are everywhere in training images.","symptom":"took 10.7 s; container exit code 137","seen":"The job has a SIGTERM handler, yet stopping it still takes the full grace period and saves nothing:","sf":"sig_wrapper","rec":[{"k":"host","f":"sig_wrapper","t":"docker run --cpus 2 --memory 3g --shm-size 256m kb-os-dbg:1 bash /exp/sig.sh wrapper"},{"k":"out","f":"sig_wrapper","t":"launcher shell is pid 1\n[   0.000s] phase start\n[   0.005s] phase import_torch\n[   1.059s] phase import_done\n[   1.061s] phase dataset_open\ndataset: 8192 rows from /work/data/train.bin (8421376 bytes)"},{"k":"host","f":"sig_wrapper","t":"docker stop (SIGTERM, then SIGKILL after 10 s) took 10.7 s; container exit code 137"}],"first":{"opts":["ps -o pid,ppid,cmd: who is PID 1?","memory.events","df -h","nvidia-smi"],"a":0,"why":"If PID 1 is a shell, the signal stops there."}},{"id":"sig_workers","title":"pkill -TERM also hits the workers: \"exited unexpectedly\" instead of a checkpoint","sub":["signal","process"],"tools":["logs","ps"],"ostep":"5 Process API","cause":"<code>pkill -f<\/code> matches every process whose command line contains the pattern, and forked workers share the main process's command line. At the start of each worker's loop PyTorch installs C-level handlers for SIGSEGV, SIGBUS, SIGFPE and SIGTERM that report the signal and then run its default action (<a href=\"https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/v2.14.1/torch/utils/data/_utils/signal_handling.py\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">signal_handling.py, NOTE<\/a>), so SIGTERM terminates the workers even though the main process handles it. The main process handled SIGTERM by setting a flag checked only at the end of a 64-micro-batch accumulation cycle, so it kept asking the dead workers for data and failed before it could save. Slurm's <code>scancel<\/code> also signals every process of a job step.","fix":"Check the flag after every micro-batch (or stop fetching once it is set); signal only the main process (Kubernetes signals PID 1 of each container); never <code>pkill -f<\/code> a training job.","ml":"The Syscall tracer tab traces the same signal arriving in the workers.","symptom":"RuntimeError: DataLoader worker (pid(s) 18) exited unexpectedly","seen":"","sf":"sig_pkill","rec":[{"k":"host","f":"sig_pkill","t":"docker run --cpus 2 --memory 3g --shm-size 256m kb-os-dbg:1 bash /exp/sig.sh pkill"},{"k":"out","f":"sig_pkill","t":"launcher shell is pid 1\nmain 13 training"},{"k":"host","f":"sig_pkill","t":"docker exec os-dbg-sig_pkill pkill -TERM -f accum.py"}],"first":{"opts":["which processes got the signal (pgrep -f lists the workers too)","df -h","memory.events","cpu.stat"],"a":0,"why":"The workers died first; ask who sent the signal to them."}},{"id":"fork_lock","title":"A worker hangs forever: fork while another thread holds a lock","sub":["concurrency","process"],"tools":["ps","py-spy"],"ostep":"27 Thread API; 32 Concurrency Bugs","cause":"<code>fork<\/code> copies the whole address space but only the calling thread (<a href=\"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/fork.2.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">fork(2)<\/a>). A lock held by another thread at that instant is copied in its locked state, and the thread that would release it does not exist in the child. The child blocks forever in <code>futex_wait_queue_me<\/code> (the kernel's wait for a futex, the primitive under Python's and pthread's locks), and py-spy shows it on the <code>with lock<\/code> line. With <code>spawn<\/code> the child starts a fresh interpreter, imports the module and creates its own unlocked lock.","fix":"Use <code>spawn<\/code> or <code>forkserver<\/code> (DataLoader's <code>multiprocessing_context<\/code>); create threads, thread pools and clients after forking workers. Python 3.12 warns when it detects fork in a multi-threaded process, and 3.14 made <code>forkserver<\/code> the default start method on POSIX instead of <code>fork<\/code> (<a href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/library/multiprocessing.html#contexts-and-start-methods\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">multiprocessing docs<\/a>); this image runs 3.11, where fork is still the Linux default.","ml":"Logging handlers, metrics clients, tokenizer and OpenMP thread pools started before the DataLoader forks.","symptom":"still stuck after 8 s (start method fork)","seen":"A DataLoader or multiprocessing worker never produces anything; no error, no CPU use:","sf":"fork","rec":[{"k":"host","f":"fork","t":"docker run --cpus 2 --memory 3g --cap-add SYS_PTRACE kb-os-dbg:1 bash /exp/fork.sh"},{"k":"out","f":"fork","t":"$ python /exp/forklock.py fork\nchild 8: taking the lock\nparent: child 8 still stuck after 8 s (start method fork)\n  PID STAT WCHAN                CMD\n    8 S    futex_wait_queue_me  python /exp/forklock.py fork\nProcess 8: python /exp/forklock.py fork\n\nThread 8 (idle): \"MainThread\"\n    child_work (forklock.py:19)\n    run (multiprocessing/process.py:108)\n    _bootstrap (multiprocessing/process.py:314)"},{"k":"out","f":"fork","t":"$ python /exp/forklock.py spawn\nchild 19: taking the lock\nchild 19: got it, working\nparent: child exited with 0 (start method spawn)"}],"first":{"opts":["ps -o stat,wchan of the stuck worker, then py-spy dump","df -h","free -m","cat cpu.stat"],"a":0,"why":"A sleeping child in a futex wait right after fork is the fork-with-threads deadlock."}},{"id":"fd_leak","title":"OSError: [Errno 24] Too many open files (a descriptor leak)","sub":["storage","limits"],"tools":["/proc","ulimit"],"ostep":"39 Files and Directories","cause":"Every open file, socket and pipe is a file descriptor, a small integer indexing the process's table of open files, and RLIMIT_NOFILE caps how many a process may hold (128 here). The Dataset opens a file per sample and keeps the file object alive, so its descriptor is never closed; at sample 124 (the limit minus the descriptors already open: stdin, stdout, stderr and a few of Python's own) <code>open<\/code> fails with EMFILE. <code>ls /proc/&lt;pid&gt;/fd | wc -l<\/code> counts them while the job runs.","fix":"<code>with open(...)<\/code>, or close explicitly; raise the limit only after removing the leak (<a href=\"https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/container/run/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">docker --ulimit nofile<\/a>).","ml":"Datasets that open shards, images or HDF5 files lazily and cache the handles.","symptom":"OSError: [Errno 24] Too many open files: '/work/samples/124.bin'","seen":"","sf":"nofile","rec":[{"k":"host","f":"nofile","t":"docker run --cpus 2 --memory 3g --ulimit nofile=128:128 --shm-size 256m kb-os-dbg:1 bash /exp/nofile.sh"},{"k":"out","f":"nofile","t":"$ ulimit -n; grep 'open files' /proc/self/limits\n128\nMax open files            128                  128                  files     "},{"k":"out","f":"nofile","t":"$ python /exp/fdleak.py 2>&1 | tail -n 1\nOSError: [Errno 24] Too many open files: '/work/samples/124.bin'"}],"first":{"opts":["ls /proc/<pid>/fd | wc -l and the limit in /proc/<pid>/limits","free -m","cat cpu.stat","nvidia-smi"],"a":0,"why":"Count the descriptors and compare with the limit."}},{"id":"fd_share","title":"\"Too many open files. Communication with the workers is no longer possible\"","sub":["ipc","limits"],"tools":["/proc","ulimit"],"ostep":"39 Files and Directories","cause":"No leak in your code this time: with PyTorch's default sharing strategy on Linux, <code>file_descriptor<\/code>, every tensor a worker sends arrives as an open descriptor to its shared-memory segment, held as long as the tensor lives (<a href=\"https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/multiprocessing.html#sharing-strategies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">sharing strategies<\/a>). Keeping 28 samples of four tensors alive used up the 128 descriptors. The <code>file_system<\/code> strategy names segments by file name instead and kept all 1024 samples with 7 descriptors open, at the price of segments that can be left behind in <code>/dev/shm<\/code> if the process dies.","fix":"Do not keep received batches (copy what you need, or <code>.clone()<\/code>); raise <code>ulimit -n<\/code>; or, as the message says, <code>torch.multiprocessing.set_sharing_strategy('file_system')<\/code>.","ml":"Evaluation loops that append every batch's outputs to a list.","symptom":"RuntimeError: Too many open files. Communication with the workers is no longer possible.","seen":"","sf":"nofile","rec":[{"k":"out","f":"nofile","t":"$ python /exp/fdshare.py file_descriptor 2>&1 | tail -n 2\nstrategy file_descriptor: failed after 28 samples with RuntimeError: Too many open files. Communication with the workers is no longer possible. Please increase the limit using `ulimit -n` in the shell or change the sharing strategy by calling `torch.multiprocessing.set_sharing_strategy('file_system')` at the beginning of your code\nopen descriptors at that point: 127"},{"k":"out","f":"nofile","t":"$ python /exp/fdshare.py file_system 2>&1 | tail -n 2\nstrategy file_system: kept 1024 samples; open descriptors now: 7"}],"first":{"opts":["ls /proc/<pid>/fd | wc -l against ulimit -n","df -h","cat memory.events","py-spy dump"],"a":0,"why":"The message is about descriptors; count them."}},{"id":"disk_full","title":"Disk full mid-checkpoint: a cryptic error and a destroyed checkpoint","sub":["storage"],"tools":["df","strace"],"ostep":"39 Files and Directories; 42 FSCK and Journaling","cause":"Saving straight to <code>ckpt.pt<\/code> opens it with O_TRUNC, so the good step-100 checkpoint is destroyed before the new one exists; the disk fills halfway through the step-200 write, leaving a file that cannot be loaded: no checkpoint at all. torch.save does not even say \"disk full\": its error is <code>unexpected pos<\/code> from the zip writer, and only strace (or <code>df<\/code>) shows the <code>write<\/code> calls returning ENOSPC. Writing to <code>ckpt.pt.tmp<\/code> first and then <code>rename<\/code>-ing it over the old one changes the outcome: the failed write leaves the old checkpoint untouched, because rename replaces the name atomically and only after the new file is complete (<a href=\"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/rename.2.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">rename(2)<\/a>).","fix":"Write-then-rename (the running job's <code>save_checkpoint<\/code>), check free space before saving, keep the last two checkpoints, and delete the partial temp file on failure.","ml":"Checkpoints are the biggest files a job writes, usually to a shared volume that other jobs fill.","symptom":"torch.load(ckpt.pt) fails: RuntimeError: PytorchStreamReader failed reading zip archive: failed finding central directory.","seen":"","sf":"ckpt_full","rec":[{"k":"host","f":"ckpt_full","t":"docker run --cpus 2 --memory 3g --tmpfs /ckpt:size=10m --cap-add SYS_PTRACE kb-os-dbg:1 bash /exp/ckpt_full.sh"},{"k":"out","f":"ckpt_full","t":"$ df -h /ckpt | tail -n 1\ntmpfs            10M     0   10M   0% /ckpt"},{"k":"out","f":"ckpt_full","t":"$ python /exp/ckpt_full.py naive\nstep 100 checkpoint: 3151999 bytes\nfree space before the step 200 save: 2088960 bytes\nstep 200 save failed: RuntimeError: [enforce fail at inline_container.cc:672] . unexpected pos 3151680 vs 3151632\nfiles now: {'ckpt.pt': 5242880, 'logs.bin': 5242880}\ntorch.load(ckpt.pt) fails: RuntimeError: PytorchStreamReader failed reading zip archive: failed finding central directory. This is an internal miniz error. If you are seeing this error, there is a high"},{"k":"out","f":"ckpt_full","t":"$ python /exp/ckpt_full.py rename\nstep 100 checkpoint: 3152093 bytes\nfree space before the step 200 save: 2088960 bytes\nstep 200 save failed: RuntimeError: [enforce fail at inline_container.cc:672] . unexpected pos 1600 vs 1494\nfiles now: {'ckpt.pt': 3152093, 'logs.bin': 5242880}\ntorch.load(ckpt.pt) works: step 100"},{"k":"out","f":"ckpt_full","t":"$ strace -f -Z -e trace=write python /exp/ckpt_full.py naive 2>&1 | grep -m 2 ENOSPC | sed -E 's/\"([^\"\\\\]|\\\\.)*\"(\\.\\.\\.)?/\"...\"/'\n[pid    36] write(5, \"...\", 5952) = -1 ENOSPC (No space left on device)\n[pid    36] write(5, \"...\", 64) = -1 ENOSPC (No space left on device)"}],"first":{"opts":["df -h on the checkpoint volume","nvidia-smi","cat cpu.stat","ulimit -n"],"a":0,"why":"An odd error during a save: check the space first, the message may not say."}},{"id":"fsync","title":"write() returned, but the data is not on disk yet: fsync","sub":["storage","memory"],"tools":["cgroup files","sysctl"],"ostep":"39 Files and Directories; 42 FSCK and Journaling","cause":"<code>write<\/code> copies data into the page cache and returns; the pages are \"dirty\" (changed in memory, not yet on the device) until the kernel's writeback threads flush them, after up to <code>dirty_expire_centisecs<\/code> (30 s) or when dirty memory passes a threshold (<a href=\"https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/sysctl/vm.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">vm sysctls<\/a>). The container's <code>memory.stat<\/code> shows 255 MiB dirty right after a 256 MiB write, 0 MiB five seconds later and 0 MiB at ten. A power loss or kernel crash in that window loses the data although every <code>write<\/code> succeeded. <code>fsync<\/code> forces it out and returns only when the device has it: the second file had no dirty pages left when fsync returned. What cannot be shown here: a real crash. This VM cannot be power-cut, and killing the container does not lose dirty pages, since they belong to the kernel, which keeps running; the measurement shows the window, not the loss.","fix":"fsync the file before renaming it, then fsync the directory so the rename itself is durable (the running job does both). On network file systems, durability also depends on the server.","ml":"A checkpoint \"saved\" seconds before a node crash can be empty or missing after reboot. The Syscall tracer tab traces the job's fsync and rename; the OS simulators tab steps through crash consistency.","symptom":"wrote 256 MiB without fsync in","seen":"Nothing visible at all until a crash: write() returned quickly and the data looked saved.","sf":"nofsync","rec":[{"k":"host","f":"nofsync","t":"docker run --cpus 2 --memory 3g kb-os-dbg:1 bash /exp/nofsync.sh"},{"k":"out","f":"nofsync","t":"$ cd /proc/sys/vm; for f in dirty_expire_centisecs dirty_writeback_centisecs dirty_background_ratio dirty_ratio; do echo $f $(cat $f); done\ndirty_expire_centisecs 3000\ndirty_writeback_centisecs 500\ndirty_background_ratio 10\ndirty_ratio 20"},{"k":"out","f":"nofsync","t":"$ python /exp/nofsync.py\nwrote 256 MiB without fsync in 0.25 s; file_dirty 255 MiB, file_writeback 0 MiB\n   5 s later: file_dirty 0 MiB, file_writeback 0 MiB\n  10 s later: file_dirty 0 MiB, file_writeback 0 MiB\n  15 s later: file_dirty 0 MiB, file_writeback 0 MiB\n  20 s later: file_dirty 0 MiB, file_writeback 0 MiB\n  25 s later: file_dirty 0 MiB, file_writeback 0 MiB\n  30 s later: file_dirty 0 MiB, file_writeback 0 MiB\n  35 s later: file_dirty 0 MiB, file_writeback 0 MiB\n  40 s later: file_dirty 0 MiB, file_writeback 0 MiB\nwrote 256 MiB in 0.23 s; file_dirty 255 MiB, file_writeback 0 MiB\nfsync took 0.14 s; file_dirty 0 MiB, file_writeback 0 MiB"}],"first":{"opts":["was fsync called before the rename? (strace -e fsync,rename)","nvidia-smi","ulimit -n","cat cpu.stat"],"a":0,"why":"A file that vanished after a crash was never forced to the device."}},{"id":"pagecache","title":"The first epoch is slower than the rest: cold and warm page cache","sub":["storage","memory"],"tools":["cgroup files","fincore","getrusage"],"ostep":"36 I/O Devices; 21 Swapping: Mechanisms","cause":"The kernel keeps file pages it has read in the page cache, so the second read of a shard is a memory copy. A file's pages were evicted per file with <code>posix_fadvise(POSIX_FADV_DONTNEED)<\/code> (<a href=\"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/posix_fadvise.2.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">posix_fadvise(2)<\/a>), which needs no privileges, unlike <code>echo 3 &gt; /proc/sys/vm/drop_caches<\/code>, which would also have emptied the cache for every other container in the VM; <code>fincore<\/code> confirms 0 bytes resident. Readahead: on a sequential pattern the kernel reads ahead in larger requests (516 device requests for 64 MiB of 4 KiB reads, 0.06 s), and with readahead switched off the same reads cost 16384 requests and 1.64 s. Through <code>mmap<\/code> a cold first touch is a major fault, and each one brought in many pages at once (4097 major faults for the 131,072 pages of 512 MiB). Honest caveat: \"cold\" here still reached 858 MiB/s, because the VM's virtual disk is a file on the laptop's SSD that macOS caches; on a real network file system the gap is much larger (next case).","fix":"Expect epoch 1 to be slower; prefer large sequential reads (shards, not millions of small files); keep readahead on for sequential formats; size node memory so the hot dataset stays cached.","ml":"Benchmarks that run once are measuring the cold cache; ones that run twice may be measuring memory.","symptom":"cold sequential read:","seen":"The first epoch reads the data much more slowly than the following ones:","sf":"pagecache","rec":[{"k":"host","f":"pagecache","t":"docker run --cpus 2 --memory 3g kb-os-dbg:1 bash /exp/pagecache.sh"},{"k":"out","f":"pagecache","t":"$ cat /sys/block/vda/queue/read_ahead_kb\n128"},{"k":"out","f":"pagecache","t":"$ python /exp/pagecache.py\nafter evicting the file: 0 bytes of it in the page cache\ncold sequential read:     858 MiB/s (0.60 s), read from the device: 512 MiB\nafter the read: 536870912 bytes in the page cache\nwarm sequential read:    3831 MiB/s (0.13 s), read from the device: 0 MiB\n64 MiB in 4 KiB sequential reads, cold, default readahead: 0.06 s, 64 MiB read from the device in 516 requests\n64 MiB in 4 KiB sequential reads, cold, POSIX_FADV_RANDOM (readahead off): 1.64 s, 64 MiB read from the device in 16384 requests\nmmap, touch every page, cold: 0.39 s, major faults 4097, minor faults 4096\nmmap, touch every page, warm: 0.05 s, major faults 0, minor faults 8192"}],"first":{"opts":["fincore the data files (how much is cached?)","nvidia-smi","ulimit -n","cat pids.events"],"a":0,"why":"Whether the data is already in memory decides whether you are timing the disk or RAM."},"bars":{"title":"Device requests to read 64 MiB in 4 KiB sequential reads, cold","unit":"requests","vals":[{"l":"readahead on","v":516.0,"g":"516","s":"default readahead: 0.06 s, 64 MiB read from the device in 516 requests","f":"pagecache"},{"l":"readahead off","v":16384.0,"g":"16384","s":"readahead off): 1.64 s, 64 MiB read from the device in 16384 requests","f":"pagecache"}]}},{"id":"slowio","title":"Slow storage: workers sleep in D state on page faults","sub":["storage","process","limits"],"tools":["cgroup files","ps","py-spy"],"ostep":"36 I/O Devices; 37 Hard Disk Drives","cause":"A read limit of 1 MiB/s on the disk (<code>--device-read-bps<\/code>, which writes the cgroup's <a href=\"https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/cgroup-v2.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">io.max<\/a>) stands in for a slow network file system. The dataset is memory-mapped, so a worker touching a row that is not cached takes a page fault and sleeps until the read completes. <code>ps<\/code> shows both workers in state D (uninterruptible sleep: waiting for I/O, not even SIGKILL interrupts it until the I/O ends) with wait channel <code>wait_on_page_bit_common<\/code>, the kernel function that waits for a page to arrive, and py-spy shows them on the <code>np.array(self.mm[i])<\/code> line of <code>__getitem__<\/code>. <code>io.stat<\/code> counts 8421376 bytes read: exactly the dataset's size. Time to first batch: 7.838 s cold against 0.029 s warm.","fix":"Stage data on local disk or memory, prefetch with more workers (they overlap I/O waits), read large sequential chunks, or cache the dataset in RAM after the first epoch.","ml":"Datasets on NFS, Lustre or a FUSE-mounted object store: low GPU use, idle CPUs, workers in D.","symptom":"wait_on_page_bit_common","seen":"Steps are slow, the CPUs are idle, workers show state D in ps:","sf":"slowio","rec":[{"k":"host","f":"slowio","t":"docker run --cpus 2 --memory 3g --cap-add SYS_PTRACE --shm-size 256m --device-read-bps /dev/vda:1mb kb-os-dbg:1 bash /exp/slowio.sh"},{"k":"out","f":"slowio","t":"$ cat /sys/fs/cgroup/io.max\n254:0 rbps=1048576 wbps=max riops=max wiops=max"},{"k":"out","f":"slowio","t":"$ ps -eo pid,ppid,stat,wchan:30,cmd | grep -E 'STAT|train.py' | grep -v grep | cut -c1-100\n  PID  PPID STAT WCHAN                          CMD\n   15     1 Sl   do_sys_poll                    python /job/train.py --steps 30 --workers 2 --start-\n   22    15 Dl   wait_on_page_bit_common        python /job/train.py --steps 30 --workers 2 --start-\n   24    15 Dl   wait_on_page_bit_common        python /job/train.py --steps 30 --workers 2 --start-\n$ py-spy dump --pid 24 2>&1 | grep -v '^Python v' | head -n 7\nProcess 24: python /job/train.py --steps 30 --workers 2 --start-method fork --no-ckpt\n\nThread 24 (idle): \"MainThread\"\n    __getitem__ (train.py:76)\n    <listcomp> (torch/utils/data/_utils/fetch.py:54)\n    fetch (torch/utils/data/_utils/fetch.py:54)\n    _worker_loop (torch/utils/data/_utils/worker.py:375)\n$ grep -E 'time to first|done at' /tmp/cold.log; grep rbytes /sys/fs/cgroup/io.stat | tr ' ' '\\n' | grep -E 'rbytes|rios'\ntime to first batch: 7.838s (start method fork)\ndone at step 30 (finished), total 10.110s\nrbytes=8421376\nrios=97"},{"k":"out","f":"slowio","t":"$ python /job/train.py --steps 30 --workers 2 --start-method fork --no-ckpt 2>&1 | grep -E 'time to first|done at'\ntime to first batch: 0.029s (start method fork)\ndone at step 30 (finished), total 1.959s"}],"first":{"opts":["ps -eo stat,wchan (D state?) and io.stat","cat cpu.stat","ulimit -n","torch.cuda.memory_summary()"],"a":0,"why":"Idle CPUs and slow steps: look for processes in D waiting on I/O."},"bars":{"title":"Time to first batch, seconds","unit":"s","vals":[{"l":"cold, 1 MiB/s","v":7.838,"g":"7.838","s":"time to first batch: 7.838s","f":"slowio"},{"l":"warm","v":0.029,"g":"0.029","s":"time to first batch: 0.029s","f":"slowio"}]}},{"id":"systime","title":"High system time: millions of tiny reads","sub":["storage","cpu"],"tools":["getrusage","strace"],"ostep":"6 Direct Execution; 39 Files and Directories","cause":"Every <code>read<\/code> is a system call: a switch into the kernel and back, plus Python's own per-call overhead. Reading 64 MiB that is already in the page cache took 64 calls and 0.01 s with 1 MiB reads, and 1,048,576 calls and 0.90 s with 64-byte reads, 0.40 s of it in the kernel (system time). <code>strace -c<\/code> counts the calls; under strace each one also costs far more (41 microseconds per call here), so use it to count, not to time.","fix":"Read in large blocks (buffered I/O, <code>f.read(1 &lt;&lt; 20)<\/code>), use memory-mapping or a columnar format, and batch small records into shards.","ml":"Datasets of millions of tiny files or records parsed with tiny reads show high <code>sy<\/code> in <code>top<\/code> and slow workers.","symptom":"read size      64 B:  1048576 read calls","seen":"top shows a large share of system time (sy) for the data workers:","sf":"systime","rec":[{"k":"host","f":"systime","t":"docker run --cpus 2 --memory 3g --cap-add SYS_PTRACE kb-os-dbg:1 bash /exp/systime.sh"},{"k":"out","f":"systime","t":"$ python /exp/systime.py\nread size      64 B:  1048576 read calls,   0.90 s wall, user  0.50 s, system  0.40 s\nread size    4096 B:    16384 read calls,   0.02 s wall, user  0.01 s, system  0.01 s\nread size 1048576 B:       64 read calls,   0.01 s wall, user  0.00 s, system  0.01 s"},{"k":"out","f":"systime","t":"$ head -c 1048576 /dev/urandom > /work/small.bin; strace -c -e trace=read python -c \"import os; fd = os.open('/work/small.bin', os.O_RDONLY); [os.read(fd, 64) for _ in range(16384)]\" 2>&1 | tail -n 5\n% time     seconds  usecs/call     calls    errors syscall\n------ ----------- ----------- --------- --------- ----------------\n100.00    0.679462          41     16408           read\n------ ----------- ----------- --------- --------- ----------------\n100.00    0.679462          41     16408           total"}],"first":{"opts":["strace -c -p <worker> (which syscall, how many)","nvidia-smi","cat memory.events","df -h /dev/shm"],"a":0,"why":"High system time means the process lives in system calls: count them."},"bars":{"title":"Seconds to read 64 MiB from the page cache, by read size","unit":"s","vals":[{"l":"64 B","v":0.9,"g":"0.90","s":"read size      64 B:  1048576 read calls,   0.90 s wall","f":"systime"},{"l":"4 KiB","v":0.02,"g":"0.02","s":"read size    4096 B:    16384 read calls,   0.02 s wall","f":"systime"},{"l":"1 MiB","v":0.01,"g":"0.01","s":"read size 1048576 B:       64 read calls,   0.01 s wall","f":"systime"}]}}],"tree":{"q":"What is the job doing?","kids":[{"a":"It died","q":"How did it die?","kids":[{"a":"Exit code 137 or \"Killed\", no Python traceback","q":"Was it being stopped (docker stop, preemption, scancel)?","kids":[{"a":"Yes, and the stop took the whole grace period","cases":["sig_pid1","sig_wrapper"]},{"a":"No, it was running normally","cases":["oom_kill","oom_score"],"note":"Read memory.events: oom_kill counts the kills."}]},{"a":"Exit code 143 right after a stop request","cases":["sig_default"]},{"a":"A Python traceback","q":"What does the message mention?","kids":[{"a":"A DataLoader worker exited or was killed","cases":["worker_oom","sig_workers","shm"]},{"a":"Shared memory, shm, or a bus error","cases":["shm"]},{"a":"Too many open files","cases":["fd_leak","fd_share"]},{"a":"MemoryError or \"can't allocate\"","cases":["memerr"]},{"a":"CUDA out of memory","cases":["cuda_oom"]},{"a":"Resource temporarily unavailable (EAGAIN) at fork or thread start","cases":["pids"]},{"a":"An odd error while saving, or a checkpoint that will not load","cases":["disk_full","fsync"]}]}]},{"a":"It is slow","q":"Where does the time go?","kids":[{"a":"The main loop waits for batches","cases":["starve","slowio","pagecache"]},{"a":"CPU limit hit: nr_throttled grows in cpu.stat","cases":["throttle","threads"]},{"a":"Many threads, many involuntary context switches","cases":["threads"]},{"a":"High system time in top","cases":["systime"]},{"a":"Processes in state D, CPUs idle","cases":["slowio"]},{"a":"Major page faults climb, swap in use","cases":["swap"]},{"a":"Only the first epoch is slow","cases":["pagecache"]}]},{"a":"It hangs","q":"Which process is stuck, and where?","kids":[{"a":"A freshly forked worker, sleeping in a futex wait","cases":["fork_lock"]},{"a":"Workers in state D","cases":["slowio"]},{"a":"The whole container ignores docker stop","cases":["sig_pid1","sig_wrapper"]}]},{"a":"Memory keeps growing","q":"Whose memory?","kids":[{"a":"Each worker's private memory (USS), RSS flat","cases":["cow"]},{"a":"Each worker's RSS, until one is killed","cases":["worker_oom"]},{"a":"Over the limit but not killed, just slower","cases":["swap"]}]},{"a":"Something is left behind","q":"What is left?","kids":[{"a":"Processes in state Z (defunct)","cases":["zombie"]},{"a":"Workers still running after the main process died","cases":["orphan"]},{"a":"A checkpoint missing or corrupt after a crash","cases":["fsync","disk_full"]}]}]}};
